{
 "item": "E4",
 "where": "Theorem 4.23",
 "paper text": [
  {
   "line": 3565,
   "text": "For the last assertion, deleting a bridge outside the paths while\nretaining its attachments preserves $\\Psi_1$ and gives a toroidal\nproper minor of $G$. Lemma~\\ref{dis:joint-test} then gives a passing\nchoice.\n\\end{proof}\n\nLemma~\\ref{dis:relevant} handles three-attachment bridges\nwith a planar relevant completion. If a bridge has none,\nthe next corollary of Lemma~\\ref{dis:joint-test} and minor-minimality shows that it is the only bridge and that\nevery path outside it is an edge.\n\n\\begin{corollary}\\label{dis:lone-bridge}\nIf a bridge $B$ has no planar relevant completion, then $G=\\Psi_1\\cup B$ and\nthe paths not in $B$ are edges.\n\\end{corollary}\n\\begin{proof}\nIf $\\Psi_1\\cup B$ were toroidal,\nLemma~\\ref{dis:joint-test} would give a planar relevant\ncompletion of $B$, contrary to the hypothesis. Thus\n$\\Psi_1\\cup B$ is nontoroidal, so $G=\\Psi_1\\cup B$ by\nminimality. An internal vertex of a path not contained\nin $B$ would then have degree two in $G$, contrary to\nthree-connectivity. Hence every such path is an edge.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\nWhen every bridge with at least two interior vertices has\nthree attachments and a planar relevant completion, the\npreceding results give finitely many possible forms for the\nbridges. Each three-attachment bridge has either one interior\nvertex or one of the forms in\nProposition~\\ref{prop:three-root-disjoint}. Each four-attachment\nbridge has one interior vertex, and at most three have the\nsame attachment set, since four would give a $K_{4,4}$ subgraph\nand hence a $K_5$ minor. We combine these forms with disjoint\ninteriors, subject to the attachment restrictions and\nLemma~\\ref{dis:counts}. A bridge containing $P_m$ includes\nboth that path and all $\\Psi_1$-bridges meeting its interior,\nso listing its possible forms also accounts for the length\nof $P_m$. The resulting enumerations are recorded\nin~\\supp{E4,~E5}.\n"
  },
  {
   "line": 4089,
   "text": "$H_{\\mathrm{simp}}$, contradicting planarity.\n\nThus all $m$ three-attachment bridges of $H$ are assigned\nto $H_{\\mathrm{rep}}$, whose attachment vertices are\n$s_k,t_k,s_x,t_y$. Contract each bridge interior to one vertex and keep only\nthese $m$ vertices, the four attachment vertices, and the\n$3m$ edges joining the two sets. This\ngives a simple planar bipartite graph with $m+4$ vertices\nand $3m$ edges. Hence $3m\\le 2(m+4)-4$,\nso $m\\le4$. Deleting one three-attachment bridge from $G$,\nif one exists, gives a toroidal proper minor. Thus $G$\nhas at most five such bridges. If it has five, no other\nbridge or edge $s_it_j$ with $i\\ne j$ can be present, since deleting it\nwould leave all five in a toroidal proper minor.\n\nBy Lemma~\\ref{dis:relevant} and\nProposition~\\ref{prop:three-root-disjoint}, the possible\nforms on a triple $\\{c_1,c_2,h\\}$, with $c_1,c_2$ in one of\n$A_1,A_2$ and $h$ in the other, are a bridge with one interior vertex,\n$T(c_1),T(c_2),T(h),S(c_1;c_2),S(c_2;c_1)$. If the triple contains an\nedge $P_i$, label it $c_1h$. Only $T(c_1),T(h),S(c_2;c_1)$\nthen remain, by\nLemma~\\ref{lem:independent-neighborhood}(a).\nThere are twelve such triples containing an edge\n$P_i$ and six containing none, giving\n$12\\cdot3+6\\cdot6=72$ labeled forms.\n\nWe enumerate assemblies of these bridges with disjoint\ninteriors, using at most three on any one triple by\nLemma~\\ref{dis:counts}, and at most five in total.\nThe other additions are at most one vertex attached to\neach set $\\{s_i,t_i,s_j,t_j\\}$ and any subset of the six\nedges $s_it_j$ with $i\\ne j$. When five\nthree-attachment bridges are present, there are no other\nadditions. We impose\nLemma~\\ref{lem:independent-neighborhood}(a) and\nLemma~\\ref{dis:four-root-isolation}, and exclude disjoint\n$s_i$--$t_j$ and $s_j$--$t_i$ paths through distinct bridges by\nLemma~\\ref{dis:opposite-order}.\nOnly $\\mathfrak T_{48}$ and $\\mathfrak T_{58}$ survive,\nand neither has a four-attachment bridge~\\supp{E4}.\n\\end{proof}"
  }
